esign` (Method, \"Data and protocol\") — **medium**, benchmark_context\n- **Anchor:** \"NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record … each game a comparison between two teams\"\n- **Why:** The NBA seasons are one of four designs and drive the count-scale finding (7 and 16 games; a single game moves the top-5 set). The only sports citation is Elo (1978), which is about chess. Without a citation for paired-comparison or BT modeling of team sports, the sports design looks unmotivated next to the well-cited LLM designs.\n- **Search topics:**\n  - Bradley–Terry paired comparison models for rating sports teams\n  - statistical rating systems for professional basketball or team sports\n\n## Considered and deferred\n- **LLM-judge vs. expert disagreement (Results, \"Expert humans vs. an LLM judge\").** The paragraph explicitly declines a causal \"judge effect\" interpretation. Zheng et al. and Dubois et al. already cite judge biases. Further judge-bias citations would add bibliography size, not support.\n- **\"Style-controlled scores\" (Limitations).** This is a single-phrase mention. Its canonical source is probably a blog post rather than a retrievable paper, so it is not worth a retrieval pass.\n- **Tie handling as half wins (Related Work).** Davidson (1970) is already cited for tie models, which is enough.\n- **Refined search variants (knapsack, relinearization, swap).** These were never evaluated, so seeking citations for them would inflate an ungrounded part of the story.\n\n## Constraints for the retrieval pass\n- Use component-literature queries only. Do not use title copies of the research question or \"follow-ups of Huang et al. (2025)\" queries; `reference_query_plan.json` forbids them.\n- Add at most 1–3 citations per target. Insert them only at the anchored sentences, with no broader rewrite.\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"Citation coverage is dense overall (all 27 bib entries cited), but three paragraph-specific gaps remain: the upper-bound/auditing positioning of the verified sizes, the corruption-robust ranking comparator family for flip perturbations, and benchmark context for the NBA sports design.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"exact, greedy, and certified algorithms for worst-case data dropping and accuracy limits of one-step influence approximations\",\n    \"rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons under adversarial corruption or poisoning\",\n    \"paired-comparison and Bradley-Terry ratings of sports teams\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"method-upper-bound-auditing\",\n      \"section\": \"method\",\n      \"priority\": \"high\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"positioning\",\n      \"reason\": \"The paper's main interpretive caveat is that every reported size is an upper bound from a one-step influence ordering, and that the true minimum 'may be smaller, possibly by a lot'. The 'What a verified size means' paragraph supports this with a single citation (low-dimensional OLS auditing). The 'Upper bounds, not minima' limitation paragraph has no citation. Reviewers who know the drop-data literature will expect the follow-up work on algorithms that tighten or certify drop-data sets and on documented failure modes of the one-step approximation. That work bears directly on how loose the reported sizes might be.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"Computing $k^\\\\star$ is a combinatorial search problem. Provable auditing is known only in special settings ... (also Discussion: 'Upper bounds, not minima')\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"failure modes of approximate maximum influence perturbation for worst-case data dropping\",\n        \"greedy or exact algorithms for finding minimal data-dropping sets that overturn a conclusion\",\n        \"robustness auditing lower bounds certificates for small data subsets in regression\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"related-work-corruption-robust-ranking\",\n      \"section\": \"related work\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"comparator\",\n      \"reason\": \"The flip perturbation is explicitly motivated as modeling 'mislabeled or adversarially cast votes'. The manipulation paragraph cites only one paper on attacks against rank aggregation (HodgeRank/spectral) plus LLM-specific vote rigging. The neighboring literature on ranking from pairwise comparisons when a fraction of the comparisons is adversarially corrupted is the natural theoretical comparator for the flip results, and it is not cited.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons with adversarial corruptions\",\n        \"data poisoning attacks on Bradley-Terry or BTL ranking from pairwise comparisons\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"method-data-sports-design\",\n      \"section\": \"method\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"reason\": \"NBA seasons are one of the four evaluation designs and drive the headline count-scale finding (7 and 16 games; one game moves the top-5 set). The only sports citation is Elo's 1978 book on chess ratings. Nothing cites paired-comparison or BT modeling of team sports, so the sports design reads as an unmotivated add-on next to the well-cited LLM designs.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record ... each game a comparison between two teams\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"Bradley-Terry paired comparison models for rating sports teams\",\n        \"statistical rating systems for professional basketball or team sports\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"How few comparisons have to be dropped or flipped to change the top-1 or top-k set of a Bradley-Terry leaderboard. The size is measured with a one-step influence (AMIP-style) search verified by exact refits, and is compared across crowdsourced votes (Chatbot Arena 140k), expert and LLM-judge annotations (MT-Bench human and GPT-4 pairs), and sports results (NBA seasons), with synthetic and permutation controls.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Bradley-Terry maximum-likelihood (ridge-regularized, ties as half wins) fitted to pairwise comparison data\",\n    \"Approximate Maximum Influence Perturbation (AMIP): first-order, influence-function ranking of rows by their effect on the leader-challenger margin\",\n    \"Exponential + binary prefix search with exact refits to verify the smallest perturbation set that changes the ranking\",\n    \"Two perturbation types (dropping rows, flipping outcomes) and two targets (top-1, top-k set)\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Random-subset perturbation baseline (the run reports the log2 size ratio vs random)\",\n    \"Influence functions and leave-one-out / infinitesimal-jackknife approximations\",\n    \"Data-dropping robustness metrics for statistical conclusions (Broderick et al.)\",\n    \"Adversarial manipulation and vote rigging of pairwise rank aggregation and crowdsourced LLM leaderboards\",\n    \"Statistical uncertainty for Bradley-Terry rankings (bootstrap / asymptotic confidence intervals) and top-K ranking theory\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Chatbot Arena human preference votes (arena-human-preference-140k)\",\n    \"MT-Bench human expert judgments and GPT-4 pairwise judgments\",\n    \"FiveThirtyEight NBA historical game records (2014 and 2015 seasons)\",\n    \"Synthetic Bradley-Terry control and permutation control\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n    \"Changing the perturbation model from dropping comparisons to flipping outcomes: flip sets are consistently smaller than drop sets, e.g. Arena top-1 46 flips vs 62 drops\",\n    \"Changing the target from top-1 to the top-k set: on Arena and NBA, top-5 set membership is more fragile than top-1 (Arena 21 vs 62 drops; NBA 1 vs 7 or 16 drops)\",\n    \"Same model responses, different evaluation design: on MT-Bench, human expert and GPT-4 judges give different fragility\"\n  ],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"Large crowdsourced leaderboards can be extremely fragile in relative terms: dropping about 0.05% of Arena-140k votes changes the top-1\",\n    \"Small expert or LLM-judge datasets need a much larger fraction to change the top-1 (about 2.7% for MT-Bench human, about 6% for MT-Bench GPT-4); sports seasons fall in between (about 0.9%)\",\n    \"Influence-guided sets are about 16x smaller than random subsets (mean log2 ratio about 4.0), so the gap between worst-case and average-case sensitivity is large\",\n    \"Fragility depends on the margin between adjacent ranks and on the comparison graph design, not only on dataset size, so a dataset-size-normalized fraction is needed for comparisons across designs\",\n    \"The refined search variants (category-count relinearized knapsack) were never evaluated because of infrastructure failures; the reported sizes are upper bounds from the AMIP search\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"approximate maximum influence perturbation dropping small fraction of data robustness\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The search used in every reported number is AMIP-style. This query retrieves Broderick et al. and follow-ups on exact or certified versions of the drop-data metric, and on where the first-order approximation fails, which is needed to describe our sizes as upper bounds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"influence functions infinitesimal jackknife approximate leave-one-out generalized linear models\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The one-step influence score is a Newton/influence approximation for a logistic-type GLM, as in Koh & Liang. This query grounds the approximation and its accuracy, including the known gaps when many points are removed together.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Bradley-Terry model maximum likelihood estimation existence MM algorithm paired comparisons\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The estimator is a ridge-regularized Bradley-Terry MLE, so we need the classic model, Zermelo/Ford existence conditions (relevant when dropped rows disconnect the graph) and Hunter's MM fitting.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"top-K ranking from pairwise comparisons sample complexity spectral MLE\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Our top-k-set targets and the margin dependence of fragility link to theory on when top-K sets can be identified from noisy comparisons (Rank Centrality, spectral MLE). Reviewers will expect this literature.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"adversarial manipulation of rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Flipping or dropping comparisons to change a ranking is a data-poisoning attack on rank aggregation. This is the closest comparator literature to the flip/drop sets, alongside the starter vote-rigging paper.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Chatbot Arena crowdsourced pairwise human preference LLM evaluation leaderboard\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Arena-140k is the main crowdsourced dataset. This query grounds the platform design, how its rankings are computed (BT with bootstrap CIs) and the known critiques of it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"LLM-as-a-judge agreement with human expert pairwise judgments biases\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"MT-Bench human vs GPT-4 judgments are our second evaluation design, and their fragility differs. Work on judge bias and on human-judge agreement explains why the two can differ.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Elo rating predictive accuracy professional sports paired comparison\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The NBA seasons are the sports-design control, which has a dense, balanced schedule. This query grounds rating systems for sports results and their link to Bradley-Terry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"confidence intervals uncertainty quantification Bradley-Terry ranking bootstrap\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Worst-case perturbation sizes are a different notion of uncertainty from the sampling-based bootstrap/asymptotic CIs that leaderboards publish. This query retrieves ranking-inference work so we can compare the two notions; the run computes no CIs on rankings itself.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any query copying the research question title, e.g. 'stability of pairwise comparison leaderboards' or 'sensitivity of Bradley-Terry rankings to data perturbation' (the task forbids retrieving the prior published study or pages citing it)\",\n    \"Queries naming 'dropping a handful of preferences' / Huang et al. follow-ups or 'who cites' style queries that could surface the forbidden paper or derivative discussions\",\n    \"Generic filler: 'machine learning benchmarking', 'robust optimization', 'scientific method', 'paper writing'\",\n    \"Chronology or search-process queries (cycle names, knapsack/relinearized variant names, harness/infra failures)\",\n    \"Queries for the untested category-count knapsack variant as if it were a validated contribution\",\n    \"Generic LLM leaderboard survey queries with no link to pairwise comparison, influence or manipulation\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
