Aggregation from Pairwise Comparisons},\n@Article{Negahban2012RankCR,\t booktitle = {Operational Research},\n title = {Rank Centrality: Ranking from Pairwise Comparisons},\t@Article{Hunter2003MMAF,\n title = {MM algorithms for generalized Bradley-Terry models},\t@Article{Giordano2018ASA,\n booktitle = {International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Statistics},\t title = {A Swiss Army Infinitesimal Jackknife},\n@Inproceedings{Elo1978TheRO,\t title = {The rating of chessplayers, past and present},\n@Article{Gao2021UncertaintyQI,\t booktitle = {Information and Inference A Journal of the IMA},\n title = {Uncertainty quantification in the Bradley--Terry--Luce model},\t@Article{Chen2017SPECTRALMA,\n booktitle = {Annals of Statistics},\t title = {SPECTRAL METHOD AND REGULARIZED MLE ARE BOTH OPTIMAL FOR TOP-K RANKING},\n@Article{Cook2000DetectionOI,\t booktitle = {Technometrics},\n title = {Detection of Influential Observation in Linear Regression},\t@Article{Davidson1970OnET,\n title = {On Extending the Bradley-Terry Model to Accommodate Ties in Paired Comparison Experiments},\t@Article{Ma2022ATO,\n booktitle = {IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence},\t title = {A Tale of HodgeRank and Spectral Method: Target Attack Against Rank Aggregation is the Fixed Point of Adversari\n@Article{Moitra2022ProvablyAO,\t booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n title = {Provably Auditing Ordinary Least Squares in Low Dimensions},\t@Article{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA,\n booktitle = {arXiv.org},\t title = {Length-Controlled AlpacaEval: A Simple Way to Debias Automatic Evaluators},\n@Article{Boubdir2023EloUR,\t booktitle = {IEEE Games Entertainment Media Conference},\n title = {Elo Uncovered: Robustness and Best Practices in Language Model Evaluation},\t@Article{Efron1979BootstrapMA,\n title = {Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife},\t@Article{Shah2015SimpleRA,\n booktitle = {Journal of machine learning research},\t title = {Simple, Robust and Optimal Ranking from Pairwise Comparisons},\n@Article{Basu2020InfluenceFI,\t booktitle = {International Conference on Learning Representations},\n title = {Influence Functions in Deep Learning Are Fragile},\t\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-10-28T23:30:00Z | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Cycle 1 idea: exchangeable-row categories mean counts on p x p stats; relinearized Frank-Wolfe/knapsack greedy over category counts, multi (insider,outsider) pair targeting, exact-refit pruning, starter AMIP fallback. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=no evaluation evidence yet.\n  - evidence: Starter program_0 uses one-shot AMIP ranking on k vs k+1 margin only; incumbent combined_score=0.0 correct=true; no runs in idea stage.\n- 2026-10-28T23:55:00Z | step=implement | scope=attempt\n  - summary: Implemented relinearized category knapsack: _build_categories/_stats_from_counts category refits, _relinearized_greedy damped knapsack steps, _candidate_pairs multi-pair targeting, _prune_counts exact pruning, _verify_rows row-level safety check, _baseline_amip starter fallback (new set used only if strictly smaller and verified).\n  - evidence: not executed; awaiting harness evaluation\n- 2026-10-28T23:40:00Z | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Cycle 2 idea: resubmit category-count relinearized multi-pair knapsack with prune + new 2-for-1 _swap_refine, governed by deterministic integer refit cap (_deterministic_refit_cap) instead of wall-clock gating; starter AMIP verified fallback. Cycle-1 attempt failed at harness infra (sandbox lease OSError) so method is untested, not refuted. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=no evaluation evidence yet (infra failure only).\n  - evidence: attempts_history.jsonl run_exit_code=1 rubric_status missing; wrapper_output.log OSError 'no owner-private directory is available for sandbox recovery'; starter code read; no runs in idea stage.\n- 2026-10-28T23:59:00Z | step=implement | scope=attempt\n  - summary: Implemented a deterministic relinearized category knapsack. Pipeline: _build_categories/_stats_from_counts for category-count p x p refits; _candidate_pairs for multi-pair first-order knapsack ranking; _relinearized_greedy for damped (0.65) relinearized steps; _prune_counts for binary-search plus single-unit exact pruning; _swap_refine for 2-for-1 unit swaps; _verify_rows for a row-level refit with 1e-7 safety. _baseline_amip provides the starter fallback, replaced only by a strictly smaller verified set. Effort comes from an integer cap from _deterministic_refit_cap (phases at 40%/75%/100% of the cap); the wall clock is only a 0.85*deadline backstop.\n  - evidence: not executed; awaiting harness evaluation\n- 2026-10-29T00:10:00Z | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Cycle 3 idea 'Baseline-seeded exact pruning plus relinearized category knapsack'. The c01 and c02 attempts both failed with the same harness infra OSError (sandbox lease) before the program ran, so the method is untested. Resubmit the category-count relinearized multi-pair knapsack, and add an explicit _prune_baseline_seed that exact-prunes the starter AMIP count vector first within the 79:Chatbot Arena~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA} and MT-Bench~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW} established crowdsourced and judge-based pairwise evaluation of chat models. Automatic judges have known biases, such as a preference for length, which can be partly corrected~\\citep{Dubois2024LengthControlledAA}. \\citet{Min2025ImprovingYM} show that a model's Arena ranking can be improved by rigging votes, and \\citet{Singh2025TheLI} document how private testing and unequal data access distort the Arena leaderboard. \\citet{Huang2025DroppingJA} report that dropping a handful of preferences can change top LLM rankings. Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator~\\citep{Ma2022ATO}. We do not claim to be the first to show that an Arena leader is fragile. Our contribution is the controlled comparison across crowdsourced, expert, LLM-judge, and sports designs under one estimator and protocol. We also contrast drop and flip perturbations and top-1 and top-$k$ targets, and we show that cross-design conclusions depend on the size scale.\n117:$\\kver$ is an \\emph{upper bound} on $k^\\star$: an explicit set of $\\kver$ rows exists whose perturbation changes the target under the exact estimator. Nothing smaller is ruled out. Computing $k^\\star$ is a combinatorial search problem. Provable auditing is known only in special settings such as low-dimensional least squares~\\citep{Moitra2022ProvablyAO}. We do not claim to solve it on real data. Instead, we check on a planted case with a known exact minimum that the instrument is not loose (below).\n126:Table~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. The four designs are crowdsourced votes from the Chatbot Arena 140k human-preference release~\\citep{Chiang2024ChatbotAA}; expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments of MT-Bench answers~\\citep{Zheng2023JudgingLW}; and NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record, the 2014 and 2015 seasons in that file's labeling, with each game a comparison between two teams. Arena ``tie'' and ``both bad'' votes and MT-Bench tie judgments become ties ($y=\\tfrac12$); NBA games have no ties. Each design contributes three cases: drop/top-1, flip/top-1, and drop/top-$k$. With the two controls this gives 17 cases. All method choices were made on disjoint development data: the Arena 55k release and the NBA 1983 season. Any final-split row whose content hash also appears there is excluded; the recorded cross-split overlap is zero. The 17 cases were then run once in a sealed evaluation under seeds 101, 102, and 103. Every episode either returns a set that the evaluator verifies, or counts as a failure; none failed. The evaluation completed and passed all of its validity gates.\n142:Sports & NBA, 2014 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}319 & 5 & one game \\\\\n143:Sports & NBA, 2015 season (FiveThirtyEight) & 1{,}311 & 5 & one game \\\\\n253:\\paragraph{Upper bounds, not minima.}\n363:All data are public. Arena: \\nolinkurl{lmarena-ai/arena-human-preference-140k} at commit \\texttt{6322995ab34d}, CC-BY-4.0. MT-Bench: \\nolinkurl{lmsys/mt_bench_human_judgments} at commit \\texttt{f7d2896d2cc5}, human and GPT-4-pair files, CC-BY-4.0. NBA: \\nolinkurl{fivethirtyeight/data} at commit \\texttt{4c1ff5e3aef1}, file \\nolinkurl{nba-elo/nbaallelo.csv}, CC-BY-4.0. Development used the Arena 55k release (commit \\texttt{18c298340948}) and the NBA 1983 season. Raw-file checksums and the content-hash split are recorded in \\nolinkurl{task/resources/final/split_manifest.json}.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cef3c6a2-5504-4f15-8e5a-67b498eb98ed","uuid":"0e38fd75-68a6-4727-831a-353def5afb62","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:12:02.877Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"@Inproceedings{Broderick2020AnAF,\t title = {An Automatic Finite-Sample Robustness Metric: When Can Dropping a Little Data Make a Big Difference?},\n@Article{Giordano2019AHS,\t booktitle = {arXiv.org},\n title = {A Higher-Order Swiss Army Infinitesimal Jackknife},\t@Article{Monari2000WithdrawingAE,\n booktitle = {Neurocomputing},\t title = {Withdrawing an example from the training set: An analytic estimation of its effect on a non-linear parameterised model},\n@Article{Alaa2020DiscriminativeJQ,\t booktitle = {International Conference on Machine Learning},\n title = {Discriminative Jackknife: Quantifying Uncertainty in Deep Learning via Higher-Order Influence Functions},\t@Article{Bradley1952RankAO,\n title = {Rank Analysis of Incomplete Block Designs: I. The Method of Paired Comparisons},\t@Article{Zheng2023JudgingLW,\n booktitle = {Neural Information Processing Systems},\t title = {Judging LLM-as-a-judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena},\n@Article{Chiang2024ChatbotAA,\t bookti