{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVZT5BBRWRoRhYUxaXcV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017TirF8Cpfg2w1xNZDisrH6","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\nbase=json.load(open('reference_query_plan.json'))\nqueries=[\n {\"query\":\"failure modes of approximate maximum influence perturbation when dropping many data points together\",\n  \"category\":\"core_method\",\n  \"rationale\":\"Target method-upper-bound-auditing (high; Method 'What a verified size means', Discussion 'Upper bounds, not minima'). The paper says every size is an upper bound from a one-step influence ordering and that the true minimum may be much smaller. That limitation paragraph cites nothing. This query looks for documented cases where the first-order AMIP ordering misses smaller drop sets. It must not return Broderick2020AnAF, Basu2020InfluenceFI, Koh2017UnderstandingBP, or the Giordano jackknife papers, which are already in the bibliography.\"},\n {\"query\":\"exact algorithms and lower-bound certificates for the minimal data-dropping set that overturns a statistical conclusion\",\n  \"category\":\"core_method\",\n  \"rationale\":\"Target method-upper-bound-auditing, the sentence 'Provable auditing is known only in special settings'. That sentence cites only Moitra2022ProvablyAO (low-dimensional OLS). Greedy or exact search methods and lower-bound certificates for worst-case data dropping would back the claim that k* is a hard combinatorial problem and that our sizes are upper bounds. Do not use Moitra2022 again.\"},\n {\"query\":\"ranking from pairwise comparisons with a fraction of adversarially corrupted comparisons\",\n  \"category\":\"baseline_comparator\",\n  \"rationale\":\"Target related-work-corruption-robust-ranking (Related Work, 'Manipulation and validity of LLM leaderboards'). The flip perturbation stands in for mislabeled or adversarially cast votes. The theory of corruption-robust BTL/rank aggregation is the natural comparator family, and it is absent. The existing Shah2015SimpleRA covers stochastic noise, not adversarial corruption, so do not use it again.\"},\n {\"query\":\"data poisoning attack on Bradley-Terry-Luce rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons\",\n  \"category\":\"baseline_comparator\",\n  \"rationale\":\"Target related-work-corruption-robust-ranking, anchored at 'Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator'. That sentence currently cites only Ma2022ATO (HodgeRank/spectral). We want 1–2 attack-side papers on BTL-type aggregators that sit next to our drop and flip sets. Exclude Ma2022ATO and Min2025ImprovingYM, which are already cited.\"},\n {\"query\":\"Bradley-Terry paired comparison model for rating sports teams from game results\",\n  \"category\":\"benchmark_context\",\n  \"rationale\":\"Target method-data-sports-design (Method, 'Data and protocol', the sentence that models each NBA game as a comparison between two teams). The NBA design produces the count-scale finding, yet the only sports citation is Elo1978 (chess). A BT or paired-comparison sports-rating reference would motivate treating games as BT comparisons.\"},\n {\"query\":\"statistical rating models for professional basketball team strength and game outcome prediction\",\n  \"category\":\"benchmark_context\",\n  \"rationale\":\"Target method-data-sports-design, second search topic. It looks for a basketball- or team-sport-specific rating reference that supports using NBA seasons as a dense, balanced-schedule design. Use it only if the previous query does not return a suitable team-sport paper. Add at most one citation.\"}\n]\nplan={\n \"status\":\"ready\",\n \"central_topic\":base[\"central_topic\"],\n \"core_methods\":base[\"core_methods\"],\n \"comparators\":base[\"comparators\"],\n \"benchmarks\":base[\"benchmarks\"],\n \"architectural_shifts\":base[\"architectural_shifts\"],\n \"result_driven_themes\":base[\"result_driven_themes\"],\n \"queries\":queries,\n \"forbidden_query_patterns\":base[\"forbidden_query_patterns\"]+[\n  \"Generic LLM-judge bias queries (deferred in draft_reference_gaps: Zheng2023 and Dubois2024 already cover it)\",\n  \"Style-controlled Arena score queries (deferred: the source is likely a blog post)\",\n  \"Tie-model queries beyond Davidson1970 (deferred: already covered)\",\n  \"Re-queries of literatures already in references.bib: Broderick AMIP, Koh influence functions, infinitesimal jackknife, Spectral MLE / Rank Centrality / top-K theory, Chatbot Arena / MT-Bench platform papers, BT CIs and bootstrap\"],\n \"failure_reason\":\"\"\n}\njson.dump(plan,open('postdraft_reference_query_plan.json','w'),indent=2)\nEOF\ncat > postdraft_reference_query_plan.md <<'EOF'\n# Post-Draft Reference Query Plan\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n**Central topic:** How few comparisons have to be dropped or flipped to change the top-1 or top-k set of a Bradley–Terry leaderboard. Sizes come from a one-step influence (AMIP-style) search verified by exact refits. The comparison covers crowdsourced (Chatbot Arena), expert and LLM-judge (MT-Bench), and sports (NBA) designs.\n\n## Scope\nThis is an incremental pass driven only by `draft_reference_gaps.{md,json}`. All 27 existing bib entries are already cited, and coverage is dense. Three paragraph-specific gaps remain, and each gets two queries (6 in total). Each target should add at most 1–3 citations, inserted only at the anchored sentences.\n\n## Queries\n\n### Target 1: `method-upper-bound-auditing` (high, positioning)\nAnchors: Method, \"What a verified size means\" (\"Computing $k^\\star$ is a combinatorial search problem. Provable auditing is known only in special settings…\"), and Discussion, \"Upper bounds, not minima\" (no citation yet).\n\n1. **`core_method`**: *failure modes of approximate maximum influence perturbation when dropping many data points together*\n   Looks for documented cases where the one-step AMIP ordering misses smaller drop sets. These would support the caveat that the true minimum may be much smaller. Skip Broderick2020, Basu2020, Koh2017 and the Giordano jackknife papers, which are already cited.\n2. **`core_method`**: *exact algorithms and lower-bound certificates for the minimal data-dropping set that overturns a statistical conclusion*\n   Adds greedy, exact or certified drop-set work next to Moitra2022 (low-dimensional OLS), which is currently the only support for \"provable auditing only in special settings\".\n\n### Target 2: `related-work-corruption-robust-ranking` (medium, comparator)\nAnchor: Related Work, \"Adversarial attacks on rank aggregation have also been analyzed as games between attacker and aggregator\" (currently Ma2022 only).\n\n3. **`baseline_comparator`**: *ranking from pairwise comparisons with a fraction of adversarially corrupted comparisons*\n   Corruption-robust BTL/rank-aggregation theory is the natural comparator for the flip perturbation, which models mislabeled or adversarial votes. Shah2015 covers stochastic noise only, so do not use it again.\n4. **`baseline_comparator`**: *data poisoning attack on Bradley-Terry-Luce rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons*\n   Attack-side papers on BTL-type aggregators to sit next to our drop and flip sets. Exclude Ma2022 and Min2025, which are already cited.\n\n### Target 3: `method-data-sports-design` (medium, benchmark_context)\nAnchor: Method, \"Data and protocol\" (\"NBA games from two seasons of the FiveThirtyEight Elo record … each game a comparison between two teams\"). The only sports citation is Elo (1978), which is about chess.\n\n5. **`benchmark_context`**: *Bradley-Terry paired comparison model for rating sports teams from game results*\n   Motivates modeling team games as BT comparisons, since the NBA design drives the count-scale finding.\n6. **`benchmark_context`**: *statistical rating models for professional basketball team strength and game outcome prediction*\n   Fallback for a basketball- or team-sport-specific reference. Use it only if query 5 returns nothing suitable, and add at most one citation.\n\n## Deliberately not queried (deferred in the gap memo)\n- More LLM-judge bias work: Zheng2023 and Dubois2024 are enough.\n- \"Style-controlled\" Arena scores: the source is likely a blog post.\n- Tie models beyond Davidson (1970).\n- The knapsack, relinearized and swap search variants: these were never evaluated.\n- Re-queries of literatures already in the bib: AMIP, influence functions and jackknife, top-K/spectral ranking, the Arena and MT-Bench platforms, BT CIs and bootstrap.\n\n## Forbidden patterns (carried over from `reference_query_plan.json`)\n- Do not copy the research-question title into a query.\n- No \"Huang et al. 2025 follow-ups\" or \"who cites\" queries.\n- No generic filler, and no chronology or infrastructure queries.\n- Do not query the untested knapsack variant as if it were a contribution.\n- If a retrieval for queries 1–2 returns Huang2025DroppingJA or pages that cite it, discard those results.\nEOF\npython3 -c \"import json;d=json.load(open('postdraft_reference_query_plan.json'));print(d['status'],len(d['queries']),[q['category'] for q in d['queries']])\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4656,"cache_read_input_tokens":22697,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4656},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cef3c6a2-5504-4f15-8e5a-67b498eb98ed","uuid":"8df75377-3c93-4101-becd-bcd94808a241","timestamp":"2026-10-29T00:12:35.397Z","request_id":"req_011CgVZT4t4D7M5vJaHZEqjm"}
